1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:09,683
Going from a C file to a running program takes four steps.

2
00:00:09,683 --> 00:00:15,800
They are the Compiler, the Assembler, the Linker, and the Loader, and their initials spell CALL.

3
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:22,843
The compiler translates C into assembly, which is what we have been doing by hand in the last few episodes.

4
00:00:22,843 --> 00:00:28,570
The assembly a compiler outputs can contain pseudo-instructions, such as mv, li, and j,

5
00:00:28,570 --> 00:00:33,166
leaving the work of expanding them to the assembler.

6
00:00:33,166 --> 00:00:37,163
The assembler reads assembly code and produces an object file,

7
00:00:37,163 --> 00:00:42,255
which holds the machine code plus information needed for linking and debugging.

8
00:00:42,255 --> 00:00:48,133
Its first job is to expand pseudo-instructions into real instructions.

9
00:00:48,133 --> 00:00:53,882
The second job is converting labels into offsets, but there is a catch, the forward reference.

10
00:00:53,882 --> 00:01:01,100
When the assembler reads the bne, it hasn't seen the definition of Skip yet, so it doesn't know how far to jump.

11
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:02,848
The fix is to scan twice.

12
00:01:02,848 --> 00:01:09,844
The first pass generates no machine code, and only records each label's address in the symbol table.

13
00:01:09,844 --> 00:01:18,238
On the second pass we generate the machine code, and now the address of Skip is known, so the offset is 0x08 minus 0x00,

14
00:01:18,238 --> 00:01:20,666
which is eight.

15
00:01:20,666 --> 00:01:27,083
But some addresses can't be worked out however many times we scan, such as calling printf in another file,

16
00:01:27,083 --> 00:01:29,444
or taking the address of static data A.

17
00:01:29,444 --> 00:01:32,531
Their final addresses aren't known until link time,

18
00:01:32,531 --> 00:01:40,066
so the assembler records these holes to be filled in the relocation table, and leaves them to the linker.

19
00:01:40,066 --> 00:01:46,932
So an object file contains a header, the code segment, the data segment, the relocation table, the symbol table,

20
00:01:46,932 --> 00:01:49,700
and debugging information.

21
00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:53,903
The linker puts several object files together into one executable.

22
00:01:53,903 --> 00:02:00,966
Step one is to join the code segments of the files end to end, and then put the data segments after them.

23
00:02:00,966 --> 00:02:01,722
Step two.

24
00:02:01,722 --> 00:02:08,437
Once the positions of the segments are fixed, with the code starting at 0x10000,

25
00:02:08,437 --> 00:02:15,366
the final address of every symbol is fixed too, for example printf at 0x10180.

26
00:02:15,366 --> 00:02:22,919
Step three is to fill in the holes one by one, using the relocation table.

27
00:02:22,919 --> 00:02:27,819
The jal at 0x10040 in foo has to jump to printf.

28
00:02:27,819 --> 00:02:37,466
Since jal uses a PC-relative offset, what goes in is 0x10180 minus 0x10040, which is 0x140.

29
00:02:37,466 --> 00:02:44,734
Branches inside a file need no change at all, because PC-relative offsets stay correct when the whole block moves,

30
00:02:44,734 --> 00:02:48,751
which is exactly the position independence from episode eleven.

31
00:02:48,751 --> 00:02:56,146
By the way, modern systems often use dynamic linking, where libraries are linked in only when the program is loaded,

32
00:02:56,146 --> 00:03:00,133
but what we describe here is static linking.

33
00:03:00,133 --> 00:03:05,116
Finally, the loader in the operating system actually gets the executable running.

34
00:03:05,116 --> 00:03:09,729
It first reads the header, to learn how big the code and data segments are,

35
00:03:09,729 --> 00:03:13,033
and creates a new address space for the program.

36
00:03:13,033 --> 00:03:20,504
Then it copies the code and data into memory, puts the command-line arguments on the stack, initializes the registers,

37
00:03:20,504 --> 00:03:22,910
and points sp at the top of the stack.

38
00:03:22,910 --> 00:03:29,766
Finally it jumps to the startup routine, which calls main, and the program starts running!

39
00:03:29,766 --> 00:03:37,674
That completes the whole journey, from C code to assembly, to machine code, and then to a program running in memory.

40
00:03:37,674 --> 00:03:43,742
Registers, memory, branches, function calls, instruction encoding, compiling and linking,

41
00:03:43,742 --> 00:03:48,300
this is the whole picture of the RISC-V part of CS61C.

